Likutay Halachos

Yoreh Dayah &mdash; Laws of Milah 5 (continued)

Continuing from the preceding, which was based upon the Torah-teaching &ldquo;And Boaz said to Ruth&rdquo; etc.,

in Likutay Moharan Vol. I, no. 65 etc. &mdash; study it there thoroughly.

This section bridges to the laws of Shulchan Aruch Yoreh Dayah, Hilchos Milah, Siman 5, Si'eef 9

(Milas Avadim &mdash; Circumcision of Slaves)

&#x2193; The main body of Likutay Halachos Milah 5 concluded at section 8 (&sect; ח), which treated the ancient chassidim who waited one hour before and one hour after prayer. The author now turns the same thematic framework &mdash; bitul, reshimu, rectification of vessels, drawing down the sealed chassadim &mdash; to illuminate the halachos of circumcision of slaves, a bridging technique characteristic of Likutay Halachos.

&#x2728; Topic: Milas Avadim &mdash; Circumcision of Slaves

And this is the aspect of the mitzvah of

milas avadim [circumcision of slaves] &mdash; for

the slave is his [master's] monetary acquisition [kinyan kaspo],

and therefore [the master] is obligated to circumcise him, to remove the

orlah from his monetary acquisition. For the

essential purpose of the mitzvah of

milah &mdash; to remove the

orlah from the holy flesh &mdash; is in order to

remove and nullify the grip of the

zuhamas ha-nachash [the venom of the serpent] from

the body, so that the body should be rectified and become a prepared and ready

vessel to receive the light of the radiance [or ha-zrichu]

of the reshimu [imprint/trace] from the

bitul [self-nullification] mentioned above. For the

entire creation of the human being was

b'gin d'yishtamod'in lay &mdash; [in Aramaic:] in

